1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:04,818
UltraQuant: four-bit KV caching for context-heavy agents.

2
00:00:04,868 --> 00:00:09,896
This is a tour of one fused decode kernel, and of what it takes to run it on three vendors.

3
00:00:11,146 --> 00:00:19,462
The same operation, a fused multi-token decode over a four-bit KV cache, now runs on AMD, NVIDIA and Intel GPUs.

4
00:00:19,512 --> 00:00:24,203
Each port is checked against reference vectors from the same quantiser, and passes.

5
00:00:24,253 --> 00:00:31,984
At K equals eight, fusing beats K independent passes everywhere: from about two point five times to six point eight times.

6
00:00:33,234 --> 00:00:35,979
Why it matters: decode is memory-bound.

7
00:00:36,029 --> 00:00:43,586
Every new token reads the whole KV cache, about one operation per byte, while these GPUs need over one hundred sixty.

8
00:00:43,636 --> 00:00:48,309
So for long-context agents, fewer KV bytes means faster tokens.

9
00:00:49,559 --> 00:00:57,068
UltraQuant stores four-bit E2M1 floats, with one eight-bit power-of-two UE8M0 scale per thirty-two values.

10
00:00:57,118 --> 00:01:04,607
That is sixty-eight bytes per head and token, against two hundred fifty-six for half precision and one hundred twenty-eight for FP8.

11
00:01:04,657 --> 00:01:07,838
About three point eight times smaller than half precision.

12
00:01:09,088 --> 00:01:13,051
Speculative decoding verifies K candidate tokens per step.

13
00:01:13,101 --> 00:01:21,251
A straightforward kernel runs K independent queries, so each re-reads every page of the cache: eight times the traffic at K equals eight.

14
00:01:21,301 --> 00:01:27,767
Our fused kernel reads each page once, with the K tokens stacked along the M dimension of the matrix multiply.

15
00:01:27,817 --> 00:01:30,085
One loaded tile serves every token.

16
00:01:31,335 --> 00:01:46,022
In Triton at batch eight and thirty-two thousand tokens, K equals eight takes 0.54 milliseconds on an MI300X against 2.22 for independent queries, and 0.42 on a B300 against 2.85.

17
00:01:46,072 --> 00:01:49,738
At K equals one there is nothing to share, so the two match.

18
00:01:50,988 --> 00:01:52,856
Now, honest positioning.

19
00:01:52,906 --> 00:02:01,181
On a B300, NVIDIA's own FlashInfer kernels for NVFP4 are faster than ours, and already fuse K-query verify.

20
00:02:01,231 --> 00:02:06,245
Our contribution is the UltraQuant format with fused verify, on three vendors.

21
00:02:06,295 --> 00:02:09,539
We found no public CUDA or SYCL port of it.

22
00:02:09,589 --> 00:02:15,250
BitDecoding-FP4 handles one query at a time, so it re-reads the cache K times.

23
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:25,368
On Qwen3-8B, FP8 and int8 are lossless in perplexity, and KIVI-4 and NVFP4 are the best four-bit-class formats.

24
00:02:25,418 --> 00:02:32,663
The UltraQuant MXFP4-style format costs about half a perplexity point, about the same as TurboQuant-4.

25
00:02:32,713 --> 00:02:42,349
NVFP4 scales through our kernel cost only 0.06, against 0.55 for MXFP4, for four more bytes per vector.

26
00:02:43,599 --> 00:02:55,661
End to end, the whole decode step is one point three to one point six times faster than fp16 on MI300X at batch eight with K equals eight, and up to two point one times on an H100.

27
00:02:55,711 --> 00:03:00,596
At batch one there is almost no gain, because attention is a small slice of the step.

28
00:03:00,646 --> 00:03:07,116
The real value is a cache three point eight times smaller, and cheap verification at large batch and long context.

29
00:03:08,366 --> 00:03:15,210
We also ported the comparison methods to real fused AMD kernels and timed them on the same MI300X shapes.

30
00:03:15,260 --> 00:03:23,953
UltraQuant is not the most accurate: QServe int4 at equal bytes and NVFP4 are better, and FP8 is faster at K equals one.

31
00:03:24,003 --> 00:03:30,724
It is faster and more accurate than TurboQuant-3, and its edge over FP8 is capacity, not latency.

32
00:03:31,974 --> 00:03:37,065
Serving level, on an MI300X with vLLM and Qwen3-8B.

33
00:03:37,115 --> 00:03:41,156
This used vLLM's own native four-bit backend, not our kernel.

34
00:03:41,206 --> 00:03:51,412
KV capacity grows from one point one million tokens in bf16 to two point two million in fp8 and three point two million in FP4, two point nine times.

35
00:03:51,462 --> 00:04:00,725
RAG F1 slips from 0.655 to 0.618, and greedy output diverges at token ten, against twenty-four for fp8.

36
00:04:00,775 --> 00:04:11,809
When agents oversubscribe the cache, FP4 serves fifty-nine tokens a second against forty-eight; plain decode is slower, 767 against 1052.

37
00:04:13,059 --> 00:04:14,002
Caveats.

38
00:04:14,052 --> 00:04:21,075
MI300X dequantises FP4 in software, and NVIDIA's vendor kernels are faster on B300.

39
00:04:21,125 --> 00:04:28,593
The serving test used vLLM's native backend, not our kernel; plain decode is slower with FP4 and quality drops.

40
00:04:28,643 --> 00:04:32,115
These are single runs, and K equals one gains nothing.

41
00:04:32,165 --> 00:04:36,928
The paper is arXiv 2606.20474.

42
00:04:36,978 --> 00:04:39,185
Code and results are in the repository.
